1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Welcome, in this in-depth analysis we are going to explore a truly complex but absolutely fascinating economic paper.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
It is titled exactly like this: mafia and elections. How does it work?

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
No big words or abstract academic concepts today, we will basically rely on raw data and research charts,

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,000
to shed light on a phenomenon that, well, often prefers to remain in the shadows, let's dive right in.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Politics and the mafia are two powers that live on the control of the same territory,

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
either they wage war on each other, or they reach an agreement.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
This quote by Paolo Borsellino is incredibly pragmatic, almost raw, I would say,

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,000
and launches us straight into today's central mystery, namely, why mafia violence explodes

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
at very, very specific historical moments.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
What if we started to read these peaks of violence not as random chaos,

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
but as a true strategy of pure economic calculation?

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Chapter 1, the timing of violence.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
It is a fact that honestly makes you jump a bit in your seat.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The numbers from the study speak clearly: violence against politicians increases drastically.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
We are talking about homicides that more than double, guess when,

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
exactly in the twelve months preceding a national election, more than double.

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
But the real question is, why does everything break out precisely in this specific time window?

18
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Let's move on to the second section, the logic of terror.

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Researchers have a technical name for all this: strategic pre-electoral violence.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
What does that mean in practice? Well, in strictly economic terms,

21
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
a homicide in this period is not an act of anger, but a signal, a very clear message,

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
it serves to terrorize the candidates of what the theoretical model defines as the "honest party."

23
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
That is, the faction opposed to the clan's interests at that moment.

24
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
It is a ruthless way of saying, the criminal organization is strong,

25
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
and is fully prepared to use force if you throw a wrench in its works.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Section 3, the clans' targets.

27
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Let's take a look at these historical data collected in Sicily from 1945 to 2013.

28
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
They show us the map and the categories of those who paid the highest price.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
We are talking about 452 victims in total.

30
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Of these, law enforcement officers and magistrates account for 138.

31
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Then we have 78 entrepreneurs, while politicians, party members, and trade unionists account for 50.

32
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Now, at first glance 50 might seem like a small number, right?

33
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
but let's pause to reflect on how few people are actively involved in politics

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
compared to the entire population.

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Seen in proportion, the statistical risk they run during electoral campaigns

36
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
is literally extremely high.

37
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
And a key point to remember, the data show that this strategy

38
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
is applied objectively, regardless of the political side involved.

39
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Section number 4, electoral rules and homicides.

40
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
But how do things change based on the rules of the democratic game?

41
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
It is fascinating to note.

42
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
In a proportional electoral system, the violence generally follows the gap

43
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
in votes on a national scale.

44
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
If the overall competition is very tight, violence tends to rise.

45
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
On the other hand, pay attention here: with a majoritarian system, the dynamic changes completely.

46
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Violence is hyper-concentrated solely and exclusively in marginal constituencies.

47
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
The most uncertain ones, in short, where indeed a handful of votes

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
makes the difference between winning, so to speak, everything.

49
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And here emerges a statistical anomaly that leaves us truly speechless.

50
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Looking at an entire century of Italian history, researchers noticed

51
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
that during the fascist dictatorship, election-related mafia violence

52
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
simply collapsed.

53
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Zero, vanished.

54
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The reason is quite simple: there were no competitive democratic elections to influence.

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
This historical fact fits perfectly with the economic theory we are examining.

56
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
This means that the criminal organization uses violence as a tool solely

57
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
and exclusively when there is a real, tangible margin of political gain from the elections.

58
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Fifth section, the impact on the polls.

59
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
At this point, the question arises naturally,

60
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
but does this terrible strategy really work on votes?

61
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Unfortunately, mathematics answers yes.

62
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Economic data reveal that a single political homicide causes, on average,

63
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
a 2.4% drop in votes for the targeted party in that particular municipality.

64
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
It is a huge, absolutely decisive impact.

65
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
To better frame the scope of this,

66
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
the paper examines a very specific historical event.

67
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The Portella della Ginestra massacre, on May 1st, 1947.

68
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Subsequent electoral data clearly show

69
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
how that extreme act

70
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
sowed such terror as to drive huge masses of votes away from the targeted parties.

71
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
A fear that literally spread like wildfire,

72
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
so much so that this voter flight effect

73
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
was clearly recorded also in neighboring municipalities

74
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
during the elections of the following year, 1948.

75
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
We reach the final part.

76
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Section 6, silence in Parliament.

77
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Here, the researchers did a truly titanic job,

78
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
they sifted through something like 300,000 pages of parliamentary speeches

79
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
using sophisticated text mining algorithms

80
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
the result is clear.

81
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
On one hand, we discover that a so-called standard homicide

82
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
prompts most MPs to speak more about the mafia problem in the chamber

83
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
to demand justice and action,

84
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
but on the other hand, here is the chilling effect.

85
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
A targeted political homicide,

86
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
occurring before the vote,

87
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
has a diametrically opposite impact on the politicians of the threatened faction.

88
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Suddenly, silence.

89
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
They talk about it much, much less.

90
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
This mathematically proves that targeted violence achieves its most subtle purpose,

91
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
it succeeds in silencing those who have suffered threats,

92
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
even once they cross the threshold of Parliament.

93
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
This entire scenario inevitably opens up a final reflection.

94
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
If it is proven that violence can act as a cold,

95
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
ruthless mathematical calculation to manipulate votes

96
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and impose institutional silence, one must wonder:

97
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
How vulnerable do today's institutions remain?

98
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Especially in the face of these systemic pressures

99
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
that economic models manage to measure to the millimeter,

100
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
but which often remain totally invisible to the general public.

101
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
It is a question that leaves us reflecting deeply on the complex

102
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and sometimes tragic architecture of democracy.